commit be3a8b75ce235c1de57e7fc483f44418c4b8c6f6
Author: Michael Still <mikal@stillhq.com>
Date:   Thu Oct 1 18:12:21 2026 +1000

    Add a kolla-ansible deployer image.
    
    Kolla-Ansible is today installed onto the deploy host with pip, and
    its Ansible collections are then fetched from Galaxy and git by
    "kolla-ansible install-deps". The result depends on what those
    sources serve on the day, on the host's Python, and on whatever is
    already in the operator's ~/.ansible/collections, which is searched
    before any system path. Two deploy hosts installed a day apart can
    therefore run different code against the same cloud.
    
    This change adds a kolla-ansible image which packages the deployment
    tool itself, in the same way as Kolla packages every service.
    
    * It is built on openstack-base, so Kolla-Ansible is installed into
      the existing venv with upper constraints, like any other service.
    * Kolla-Ansible comes from a new kolla-ansible source, which is the
      usual tarballs.opendev.org branch tarball. The openstack.kolla
      collection comes from a new git plugin,
      kolla-ansible-plugin-ansible-collection-kolla, since that project
      does not publish tarballs. Both have kolla_build_sources and
      required-projects entries, so CI builds them from Zuul's checkouts
      and Depends-On works.
    * The collections in requirements-core.yml, and the openstack.kolla
      collection from the plugin directory, are installed at build time
      into /usr/share/ansible/collections, and ANSIBLE_COLLECTIONS_PATH
      makes that the only path searched. Nothing is fetched when the
      image runs, and nothing in a user's home directory can shadow what
      the image was built with.
    * The Kolla-Ansible version, the git commit of each source tree where
      one is known, and the installed collections are recorded in
      /etc/kolla-ansible/deployer.json. Labels cannot be computed from the
      output of a build step, so the image carries only static labels:
      kolla_ansible_launcher_protocol, which a wrapper script can check
      before running the image, and openstack_release.
    * nsenter is given cap_sys_admin, cap_sys_ptrace and cap_sys_chroot as
      file capabilities, so that the community.docker.nsenter connection
      plugin can reach the host from an unprivileged container user.
    * HOME is a world writable, sticky /var/lib/kolla-ansible, so that
      the image can run as an arbitrary UID, and an SSH client is
      installed.
    * The entrypoint is kolla-ansible itself, so that
      "docker run kolla-ansible --help" works without any wrapper.
    
    Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context, high effort) <noreply@anthropic.com>
